  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+20 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+20 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+20 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-4}{2}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-8x+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-4)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{x-4}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
